\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Type--mechanism dissociation and intervention design.}
The central finding is a type--mechanism dissociation: type predicts failure mechanism more strongly than violation rate (Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$), reframing the taxonomy from risk classifier to diagnostic instrument. Of the 60.8\% residual ($\sim$15\% stochastic, $\sim$46\% systematic; Appendix~\ref{app:residual}), the systematic component reflects clause-level interactions that type-level taxonomies cannot capture, motivating clause-level auditing (even perfect disambiguation addresses $\leq$22.4\% of variance). Prompt-based mitigation is type-dependent (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}), reinforcing policy-design over prompt-level fixes. Violation severity achieves perfect human $\kappa = 1.00$ while mechanism classification reaches only $\kappa = 0.15$ (collapsed: $0.36$), confirming that \emph{what} constitutes a violation is clear but \emph{why} it occurs is interpretively harder. Authorization scope is a construct outlier ($\kappa = 0.385$, 52\,pp range)---its low inter-judge reliability reflects genuine construct difficulty rather than taxonomy invalidity; the core type--mechanism mapping (boundary violation, 5/5 models) remains the most unanimous in our data (Appendix~\ref{app:sans_auth}).

\paragraph{Ecological validity.}
This paper identifies causal mechanisms, not deployment-ready effect sizes. The SOPBench replication ($+$12.7\,pp vs.\ $+$34.0\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}) confirms directional consistency; the $2.7\times$ compression reflects three measurement factors: (1)~grain (constraint- vs.\ episode-level scoring), (2)~baseline compression (22.7\% vs.\ 6.9\% unambiguous rates), and (3)~interaction depth (single- vs.\ multi-turn). Cross-domain type rankings show modest correlation ($\rho = 0.26$; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_domain}), indicating that type-level findings are domain-contingent. The dissociation depends on mechanism structure, not magnitude, generalizing independently of effect size calibration.

