\section{Error Analysis \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:analysis}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_type_mechanism.pdf}
\caption{Type--mechanism association (percentage of violation traces per type assigned to each failure mechanism). Each type maps to a dominant mechanism ($\geq$3/5 model consistency), supporting the taxonomy's diagnostic value (NMI $= 0.23$, Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$).}
\label{fig:type_mechanism}
\end{figure}

The following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from LLM-coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

We analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes (Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$, $\kappa = 0.52$ \citep{gwet_2014,cohen_1960}). The failure mode coder prompt receives the agent trajectory, policy clause, ground truth, and violation assessment, but does \emph{not} include the ambiguity type label; type is assigned programmatically post hoc. Using LLMs to code LLM failures introduces circularity risk; however, mutual information between type (exogenous) and mechanism labels ($I(\text{type}; \text{mechanism}) = 0.41$ bits, $V = 0.45$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}) provides structural evidence against this. Human mechanism coding yields $\kappa = 0.15$ (Appendix~\ref{app:human_validation}), concentrated on the spurious compliance vs.\ structural misparse boundary; collapsing these into a single \emph{policy misinterpretation} category raises $\kappa$ to $0.36$. The type--mechanism association reported here ($V = 0.45$) should therefore be interpreted as an upper bound; the directional pattern---type predicts mechanism more strongly than severity---holds under both LLM and collapsed human coding. The following failure mode patterns should therefore be interpreted as consistent LLM categorization tendencies rather than ground-truth mechanism assignments. Two of the six type--mechanism mappings reflect partial definitional affinity (authorization scope$\to$boundary violation, incompleteness$\to$gap-filling); the remaining four (e.g., scopal$\to$structural misparse, 82.7\% cross-model consistency) are empirically derived.

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}

The five-model data reveal three distinct failure patterns (as classified by LLM coders) that crosscut the specification/linguistic layer boundary.

\emph{Structural misparse}: the agent commits to an incorrect parse, paralleling garden-path processing \citep{frazier_rayner_1982,ferreira_good_enough_2002}. Dominates scopal (5/5), conditional precedence (4/5), and coreferential (3/5).

\emph{Information gap-filling}: the agent fills missing information with plausible but incorrect defaults. Dominates incompleteness (3/5) and lexical (3/5).

\emph{Boundary violation}: the agent exceeds permission boundaries when authorization scope is underspecified; 69.2\% involve unauthorized escalation with zero conservative refusals ($p < 10^{-4}$, binomial). Dominates authorization scope exclusively (5/5).

All dominant modes achieve $\geq$3/5 model consistency; scopal and authorization scope are unanimous (5/5).

\subsection{Disambiguation Neglect}
\label{sec:dn}

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity. Overall awareness is 4.6\%, clustering by model: GPT-4.1 and Claude show 10--11\% while Qwen3 and DeepSeek exhibit zero across 278 episodes ($p = 2.2 \times 10^{-9}$; Appendix~\ref{app:awareness_breakdown}).

We term this \emph{disambiguation neglect}---extending the ambiguity collapse phenomenon documented in static NLP tasks \citep{liu_ambiguity_2023,hu_ambiguity_2025} to agentic policy execution, where it produces irreversible safety violations rather than mere accuracy degradation. A prompt-based mitigation (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}) confirms type-dependent effectiveness: authorization scope ($-$18.0\,pp), lexical ($-$8.9\,pp), but not incompleteness ($-$3.4\,pp, n.s.), reinforcing policy-design over prompt-level fixes.

\subsection{Systematic Nocuity}

We adopt `nocuous' from \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}, but inverted: our agents exhibit \emph{convergent} rather than \emph{divergent} errors. Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models; 13.0\% unanimous)---we term this \emph{systematic nocuity}, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. Of these, 83.9\% share the dominant failure mode, confirming convergent---not coincident---violations (incompleteness highest: 24\%, OR $= 2.61$; subject to information-addition confound).

These convergent violations exhibit a \emph{permissive attractor}: 77.0\% reflect permissive interpretation ($p \approx 0$; Appendix~\ref{app:attractor}); conditional precedence is the exception (51.5\% permissive vs.\ 47.1\% restrictive).

